course: adult-life
lesson: 58
title: Landing on an 18 film by accident: how it happens and how it feels
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes a child sees a few seconds or minutes of a film made for adults without meaning to. It happens to lots of children, and it is not their fault: screens are full of grown-up things, and one wrong tap or a late channel is enough.
explain: It usually happens in ordinary ways: autoplay starting the next video, a grown-up's profile on the streaming app, a link in a group chat, a late-night channel, an old DVD in a box, an advert, or an older child at a sleepover who puts a film on.
explain: Vivid example: in Dublin, Alfie's cartoon ends on the family tablet and, before he can move, the next video starts by itself: a trailer marked 18. His heart thumps, his cheeks go hot, and he shoves the tablet under a cushion. Later he can't stop thinking about it, and he isn't sure if he's in trouble.
explain: Afterwards children feel all sorts of things: a racing heart, pictures that come back at bedtime, curiosity, shame, anger, the wish to keep it secret. All of these are normal, they fade with time, and they get smaller much faster once you tell a grown-up you trust.
fact: Seeing part of an 18 film by accident is common and is not the child's fault.
fact: Accidents happen through autoplay, other people's profiles and devices, links in chats, search results, adverts, late-night channels, old discs, and older children choosing the film.
fact: Autoplay starts the next video on its own, and the next one may not be meant for children.
fact: A grown-up's or teenager's profile on a streaming app shows films for their age, not yours.
fact: When you see something upsetting, the body reacts fast: the heart races, the cheeks go hot, you may freeze.
fact: A picture you saw for a second can come back in your head, often at bedtime; this is normal and fades, especially after talking about it.
fact: Wondering what was in the rest of the film is natural; the safe way to deal with the wondering is to ask a trusted grown-up, not to go looking.
fact: Keeping it a secret usually makes the feeling heavier; telling makes it lighter.
fact: Feeling embarrassed is normal; grown-ups have heard it before and will not think less of you.
fact: Feeling angry at the tablet, the channel or the friend who showed it is understandable; the screen did something you didn't choose.
fact: Seeing a bit of an adult film does not make you grown up or change who you are.
fact: Never touching the remote again is not the answer; knowing what to do next time is.
fact: Switching off or walking away as soon as you see an 18 is always allowed, even at someone else's house.
exercise: Freya can't sleep, flicks through channels late at night and lands on a film marked 18. Is it her fault? | No, it was an accident; switch off and tell | Yes, she should be punished for a month | Yes, she turned it on, so it's all her fault | No, so she may as well keep watching
exercise: Alfie's cartoon ends and an 18 trailer starts by itself. What made that happen? | Autoplay, which starts the next video on its own | Alfie's thumb, which pressed play without him knowing | The cartoon makers, who wanted to scare him | A virus, which takes over tablets at night
exercise: Grace borrows her teenage cousin's tablet and an app opens with an 18 film paused. Why? | The tablet was set up for an older person | Tablets choose films at random to surprise you | Her cousin left it there as a trick to play on her | Apps always open on the most recent 18 film
exercise: Jake logs into his big brother's streaming profile and sees an 18 film at the top. Why? | Each profile shows films for its owner's age | The app shows everyone 18 films first, whatever their age | His brother made that film himself | Films at the top are always for kids
exercise: Ruby taps a video link in the group chat and it opens an 18 trailer. What's the lesson? | Links can lead anywhere; tell a grown-up | Links are always safe if a friend sent them to you | It's her fault for having a group chat | She should send the link to more friends
exercise: Harry finds an old 18 DVD at Grandma's and puts it in. What should he have done first? | Checked the rating on the box | Turned the volume right down first | Asked his little sister to watch | Fast-forwarded to the ending
exercise: At a sleepover, a friend's big sister puts on an 18 film for the group. Can Poppy leave the room? | Yes, she can always leave or ask to ring home | No, it would be rude to the big sister | No, she has to stay with the group | Yes, but only if everyone else leaves too
exercise: Max's dad falls asleep and the channel starts an 18 film. What's the best thing to do? | Switch it off and wake Dad | Keep watching quietly so Dad sleeps | Turn the volume up to wake Dad | Leave the room and say nothing
exercise: On the bus, Sophie's friend shows her a bit of an 18 film on her phone. What can Sophie say? | "No thanks, that's not for me," and look away | "Show me more," so she isn't left out | "Send it to me," to watch at home later | Nothing, she has to watch to be polite to her friend
exercise: Theo is watching football online when an advert for an 18 film pops up. Whose fault is it? | Not his; adverts can pop up uninvited | His, for watching sport on a website | The footballers', for being in the match | His, for not shutting his eyes in time
exercise: Lily searches for a cartoon and an 18 film with a similar title appears. What happened? | The search mixed up two titles that sound alike | Lily typed something rude without meaning to | The cartoon was secretly an 18 all along | Search engines only show adult films first
exercise: Oscar's heart races after he lands on an 18 film for a few seconds. Is something wrong with him? | No, a racing heart is the body's normal alarm | Yes, he should go straight to hospital in an ambulance | Yes, it means he liked what he saw | No, his heart always races at films
exercise: A picture Evie saw for a second keeps coming back at bedtime. What's true? | It's normal, and it fades, faster if she talks about it | It means the picture is stuck there for life | It means she must watch the whole film to stop it | It's a sign that she is ill and needs medicine
exercise: Finn switched it off fast but keeps wondering what was in it. What's the safe way? | Ask a trusted grown-up his questions | Search for the film later when no one's looking | Ask the older kids at school to tell him about it | Pretend he isn't wondering about it at all
exercise: Ava saw an 18 film on her cousin's tablet and decides to keep it a secret. What usually happens to a secret like that? | It feels heavier, until she tells someone | It disappears on its own after one night | It turns into a nice memory with time | It makes her cousin feel much better
exercise: Leo is too embarrassed to tell Dad why he's quiet. What's true about embarrassment? | It's normal; grown-ups have heard it all before | It means Leo did something very wrong and bad | Grown-ups will laugh at him if he tells | It's a reason to keep quiet forever
exercise: Mia is cross at the tablet for showing her something she didn't want. Is her anger fair? | Yes, it showed her something she didn't choose | No, tablets can't show anything by themselves | No, she should be cross only with herself | Yes, so she should throw the tablet away
exercise: Noah feels he has "grown up too fast". What would a good parent tell him? | A few seconds of a film doesn't change who you are | Yes, he is now an adult and must act like one from now on | He should now be allowed to watch all 18s | He has lost his childhood and can't get it back
test: Isla can't sleep after seeing a few seconds of an 18 film at a friend's house. Her big brother: "Just forget it, don't tell Mum, she'll go mental." Put him right. | Telling Mum helps; good parents don't blame you | He's right, telling grown-ups always makes things much worse | Isla should watch the whole film to forget it | Isla should tell the friend's mum to ring Mum
test: Charlie, after an accident with a late film: "I'm never touching the remote again!" What's better? | Learn what to do next time | Make sure no one ever watches TV | Hide the remote under the floor | Only watch TV with eyes half shut
test: Mum on the phone: "Apparently the older girl just stuck it on, the little ones didn't have a clue what it was." What happened at the sleepover? | An older child put on a film the younger ones didn't choose | The little ones picked an 18 film themselves and hid the box from her | The older girl stuck a poster on the wall | Nobody watched anything at the sleepover
test: A boy at school: "If you've seen an 18 you're basically an adult now." What's the truth? | Seeing it doesn't make anyone grown up | He's right, it counts just like a birthday | It makes you an adult for one year | It means you can now buy 18 films
test: Older cousin: "Crap, forgot to log out, sorry, that's my profile." What went wrong? | His profile showed films for his age, not yours | His profile was broken and showed random films | He logged out, so the app went haywire | Logging out shows every film at once
test: Grandad, finding the DVD in the player: "Blimey, that's been in the box since the nineties." Why was it in Grandma's house? | Grown-ups once bought it for themselves | It was bought for Harry as a birthday present | Old DVDs turn into 18s with age | It came free with a box of cereal
test: Your friend: "I saw it for like a second and now I keep seeing it when I close my eyes, am I weird?" What's the kind, true answer? | No, that's normal, it fades, and talking helps | Yes, nobody else in the world has ever had that happen | No, but you should watch it again to stop it | Yes, you should keep it a secret forever
test: Dad, grumbling at the telly: "Who on earth puts an advert like that on during the footy at teatime?" What is he cross about? | An advert unsuitable for children came on | The football match finished far too early | The TV was too loud during teatime | His team lost because of the advert
test: Big sister: "If it came on the TV, it's your fault for having the TV on." Put her right. | It's an accident, not his fault | She's right, TV is always risky | It's Mum's fault for buying a TV | It's the fault of whoever made the TV
test: Lily's search for a cartoon showed an 18 film. "Now Mum'll think I was looking for it!" What's most likely? | Mum will understand titles get mixed up in searches | Mum will decide Lily is lying about the cartoon and was looking | Mum will ban Lily from all cartoons forever | Mum will think the cartoon was the 18 film
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child may encounter an adult film without intending to do so. | Autoplay, shared devices, links or late broadcasts | A wish on the child's part to behave badly in secret | A requirement imposed by the school curriculum | A legal obligation upon all streaming services
exam: Determine the assessment which this lesson commends in respect of a child who has seen an adult film by accident. | That the child is not at fault for the accident | That the child is wholly to blame for the incident | That the child has thereby become an adult | That the child must view the remainder of the film
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning images which recur in a child's mind after such an incident. | They are normal, and diminish over time and with discussion | They are permanent and cannot be diminished by any means | They indicate that the child is suffering from an illness | They may be removed only by viewing the film in its entirety
exam: Ascertain the course of action which most readily alleviates the distress occasioned by an accidental viewing. | Informing a trusted adult of the incident | Concealing the incident from all persons | Seeking out the remainder of the material | Abstaining from all television permanently
